  "body": {
    "id": "prop:two-arm-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "When \\(K=2\\), \\(m=1\\), \\(C=(-1,1)\\), and \\(W=(1)\\), the cone condition holds and \\(Y^{\\uparrow}(F)\\) yields exactly the comonotone sharp upper randomization-variance endpoint of Aronow, Green, and Lee.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "K",
      "m",
      "C",
      "W",
      "Y^{\\uparrow}(F)",
      "F"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:balanced-complete-randomization",
      "ass:causal-contrast",
      "thm:rank-cone-iff"
    ],
    "justification": "The reduction is the required sanity check against the canonical sharp two-arm result.",
    "gap": "This proposition is intentionally an equivalence check rather than a novelty claim.",
    "consumer": "Existing two-arm causal-bootstrap software becomes a verified special case of the general certificate.",
    "proof_tex": "Under the stated values,\n\\[\n H=C^{\\mathsf T}WC=\n \\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\\\-1&1\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathcal R_2.\n\\]\nHence \\(\\texttt{thm:rank-cone-iff}\\) proves that common ranks maximize \\(\\rho_W\\).  Under the stated scalar choices, \\(\\rho_W(Y)\\) is exactly\n\\(\\operatorname{Var}_Z\\{\\widehat{\\bar Y}(2)-\\widehat{\\bar Y}(1)\\mid Y\\}\\).  The maximum is sharp because \\(Y^{\\uparrow}(F)\\) belongs to the completion class and attains it, and it is comonotone because it pairs the two marginal order-statistic columns in their common increasing order.  This is exactly the two-arm endpoint named in the proposition.",
